{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Et ha-berakhah</b> — in \"berakhah\" he said \"et\" in the language of inclusion, for not one thing fell from all His good word; but in the curse he did not say \"et\" but rather \"ve-ha-kelalah.\" Another interpretation: <b>et ha-berakhah</b> — from alef to tav, from \"im be-chukotai\" until \"komemiyyut,\" and in the curse from vav to heh, from \"ve-im lo tishme'u\" until \"ve-ein koneh.\" And in the curse he said \"im lo tishme'u\" and in the blessing he said \"asher tishme'un\" and did not say \"im\" in the language of doubt, but said \"asher\" in the language of \"ashrei,\" as it is said: \"Ashrei ha-adam shome'a li.\"" "<b>Et ha-berakhah asher tishme'u</b> — sot תורה: in the merit of the Torah the blessings will come."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase \"ve-ein koneh\" likely refers to the end of the tokhachah in Leviticus 26 (v. 40ff.), though the exact lexical match is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:09:29.370803009Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}